          "inlines": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "In a criminal trial, it is easy to think that strong evidence against an accused person must mean they are very likely guilty. But is this always true?",
              "marks": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "p17-q19-definitions-paragraph",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "fontSize": null,
          "align": "left",
          "spaceBefore": 0,
          "spaceAfter": 1,
          "lineHeight": 1.4,
          "indent": 0,
          "inlines": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Let ",
              "marks": []
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "latex": "G",
              "display": false
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " be the event that the accused is guilty, and let ",
              "marks": []
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "latex": "E",
              "display": false
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " be the event that a particular piece of evidence is found (for example, the DNA of the accused matches DNA found at the crime scene).",
              "marks": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "p17-q19-response-prompt",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "fontSize": null,
          "align": "left",
          "spaceBefore": 0,
          "spaceAfter": 1,
          "lineHeight": 1.4,
          "indent": 0,
          "inlines": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "If ",
              "marks": []
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "latex": "\\Pr(E\\mid G)",
              "display": false
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " is large, does this necessarily mean that ",
              "marks": []
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "latex": "\\Pr(G\\mid E)",
              "display": false
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " is also large?",
              "marks": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": {
        "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
        "version": 1,
        "blocks": [
          {
            "id": "p17-q19-short-result",
            "type": "paragraph",
            "fontSize": null,
            "align": "left",
            "spaceBefore": 0,
            "spaceAfter": 2,
            "lineHeight": 1.4,
            "indent": 0,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "No, not necessarily.",
                "marks": []
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "p17-q19-short-reason",
            "type": "paragraph",
            "fontSize": null,
            "align": "left",
            "spaceBefore": 0,
            "spaceAfter": 2,
            "lineHeight": 1.4,
            "indent": 0,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "By Bayes’ rule, the probability of guilt given the evidence also depends on the prior probability of guilt and the probability of the evidence.",
                "marks": []
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      "worked": "No, not necessarily. By Bayes’ rule,\n$$\\Pr(G\\mid E)=\\frac{\\Pr(E\\mid G)\\Pr(G)}{\\Pr(E)}.$$\nA large $\\Pr(E\\mid G)$ does not guarantee a large $\\Pr(G\\mid E)$: the ratio $\\frac{\\Pr(G)}{\\Pr(E)}$ also matters. If the prior probability $\\Pr(G)$ is very small relative to $\\Pr(E)$, the probability of guilt given the evidence can still be small. Confusing these conditional probabilities is the prosecutor’s fallacy.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 32
